many syrian refugees come to the densely  packed buildings of the jordanian  capital amman seeking refuge from the  bloody civil war raging in their own  country  jordan has long been a destination for  those escaping violence in neighboring  countries  over the years palestinians and iraqis  have flocked here now it's mainly  syrians who are coming  an estimated 750 000 of them  around two-thirds live outside refugee  camps many of them in oman  hospitality is part of jordanian culture  but the refugees are a topic of  discussion  we must take them she says where else  can they go  every country has good people and bad so  they're welcome here but well some are  nice and some aren't  the refugees have caused all sorts of  problems says this man from traffic to  housing and everything's more expensive  second  we want to find out what challenges are  facing syrian refugees and their  jordanian hosts  there are strong cultural and economic  ties between the two countries many  syrians have emigrated to jordan in the  past they've invested in local companies  and started their own businesses  but not every syrian refugee can count  on help from relatives who live in  jordan  finding a job isn't easy  and work permits are hard to come by  this is marca amman's industrial quarter  we've heard that many syrians work here  the owner of this workshop is willing to  talk to us  he knows the problems that syrian  workers have and the effect they have on  the job market  jordanian law forbids syrians from  working unless they have a permit he  says  but even so some businesses have let  jordanians go and hire syrians  on the market  he too employs syrian workers and not  just because they're cheaper  there's a big difference he says  syrians like to work  jordanians often aren't hard workers  this young syrian worked as a mechanic  before coming to jordan  he's employed here too even though he  doesn't have a work permit  not everyone would allow it he says  the young man's journey to amman wasn't  easy  he spent months living in the zatari  refugee camp near the northern border  a relative had to pay for him to be  released  now he's finally found work  it's the same story for many syrian  refugees coming to jordan means coming  to the zatari camp  we travel there to meet some of them  around 100 000 people live here in the  middle of the desert  for more than a year this u.n run  refugee camp has been the first  destination for syrians who managed to  make the dangerous journey across the  border  in the middle of the camp is a bakery  ibrahim and his family fled here from  daraa a year ago they owned a bakery  back in syria  when we arrived there was nothing but  pre-prepared food here says ibrahim  there was no bread nothing to buy  my brother said i should start baking  again  we had to smuggle in the equipment and  the cooking gas it was dangerous  we worked out of a tent when we started  we sold flatbread and falafel  now the market has developed and we run  this bakery  the business is going well  the earnings are enough to support nine  families  but it's not easy  in order to get flour and other  ingredients ibrahim has to rely on  jordanian suppliers and officials  his brother-in-law suhir is frustrated  with the situation  everyone wants bribes he says the un  people the jordanian military everyone  the whole family works in the bakery  even the children they haven't been  going to school for the past two years  despite the difficulties the family is  proud to have carved out a niche but  it's still hard for ibrahim to  contemplate the future  i have no idea he says  i don't really think about going back to  syria  the bakery is just one of many shops  that have opened on the main street of  the refugee camp it seems that many here  are not counting on going back to syria  soon  but not everyone is happy to stay many  want to start new lives outside the camp  we travel a few kilometers to the border  town of mafraq  until a year ago the town had seventy  thousand residents  now there are one hundred twenty  thousand  a beer  and her family belong to the large group  of refugees who have chosen to seek  security here  the family used to live in the zatari  camp a beer still misses her old home  and homes  our old home was different she says  imagine our kitchen was tiled  the apartment had windows and wooden  doors  it had louvered blinds  she says there's still lots of cleaning  to do  the apartment is simple but not cheap  rents have quadrupled in the past year  the family has to pay the equivalent of  190 euros per month  apartments are scarce because of the  crisis the cost puts strain on abeer's  family especially since her husband  isn't working  the family of six can't afford to spend  much on food they get basics from the  united nations  they've managed to get mattresses and a  tv  besides practical problems they're still  coming to terms with the horrors of the  war  two days before we left he says a lot of  planes came and bombed the mosque  and lots of people died  as always it's the children who suffer  most from the trauma of war  they get some relief by going to school  every afternoon ahmed and his siblings  attend a private school set up by the  charity karitas  many jordanian schools are now teaching  in the morning and afternoon to  accommodate the syrian children  the children have to get used to having  regular lessons again the teachers say  lots of them have missed months of  school because of the conflict  this teacher tells us that the children  were loud and unruly when they first  arrived  they were scared and traumatized she  says  now they like the school and when we  finish in the evening they don't want to  stop  learning one knows how long these  refugees will have to endure their  ex-island jordan  many have decided to build new lives  here  we set off back to amman  there we meet with bayan al-adawi a  syrian activist who helped found a  women's initiative called syrian kitchen  the members meet discreetly in an out of  the way house they're careful not to  attract too much attention because many  still have family in syria  in the kitchen the women bake and cook  syrian specialties  the refugees would like to set up a  catering service for jordanian clients  they say they're ready to start new  lives  they should feel as though they're  members of society says  they don't want to just wait for help to  be given to  the them kitchen project helps the women  and they realize the work they do is  worthwhile  we've had a great response especially  from our customers  the project is still in its early phases  the women already have a few jordanian  customers  they rely on word of mouth  everyone here is worried about the  future and about their families back in  syria  that's another reason they don't want  their faces shown  my relatives are spread all over says  this woman in egypt turkey lebanon and  here  my family's torn  apart it's unbelievable that bashar  al-assad is still in power  after the bloodbath after all the deaths  how can he live with himself  and how can he still be ruling syria  despite the difficulties faced by the  refugees life goes on how long they'll  be away from their homeland is  impossible to tell  but until they can return many syrians  will be staying in their adopted country  jordan
 

